      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that's a good start. On the screen reader side, I'm particularly concerned about how we handle the more complex elements in the dashboard, like the dynamic data tables and the real-time transaction feeds. We use a lot of AJAX updates that change content without a full page reload. How can we make sure those updates are communicated to screen reader users so they aren't left in the dark about what's happening on the screen?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The age-old problem of keeping screen reader users in the loop, especially when it comes to dynamic content updates. It's a bit like trying to navigate a dark cave without a torch - you need to make sure you're not leaving anyone behind.\n\nTo address this, you'll want to implement some screen reader-friendly techniques for handling dynamic content updates. Here are a few strategies to consider:\n\n1. **ARIA Attributes:** Use ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) attributes to provide screen reader users with a clear understanding of what's happening on the screen. For example, you can use `aria-live` to indicate that a dynamic element is about to change, and `aria-atomic` to ensure that the screen reader updates in real-time.\n2. **Screen Reader-Specific ARIA Attributes:** Some screen readers, like JAWS and NVDA, support additional ARIA attributes that can help with dynamic content updates. For example, you can use `aria-atomic` to indicate that a dynamic element is about to change, or `aria-relevant` to specify which parts of the element are relevant to the screen reader user.\n3. **AJAX Updates with Screen Reader-Friendly Content:** When updating dynamic content, make sure to include a",
      "source": "target",
