      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those pesky, unpredictable behaviors can be like a swarm of mischievous goblins, flitting about and causing trouble. But, just like how you can outsmart a goblin or two, you can also document and categorize these behaviors to help your engineers track them down.\n\nTo tackle this, I recommend creating a \"Goblin Log\" or a \"Behavioral Anomaly Database\" to document and categorize these unpredictable behaviors. Here's a suggested framework to get you started:\n\n1. **Goblin Log Entry Template:** Create a standardized template for logging each incident, including:\n\t* Date and time of occurrence\n\t* User agent and browser type (if applicable)\n\t* Screen reader software used (if applicable)\n\t* Description of the behavior (glitch, disappearance, etc.)\n\t* Any relevant screenshots or videos\n\t* Any error messages or logs\n2. **Categorization:** Develop a set of categories to group similar behaviors together, such as:\n\t* **Glitchy Behavior:** Elements that appear to glitch or freeze for a brief period\n\t* **Disappearances:** Elements that suddenly vanish from the screen\n\t* **Unpredictable Updates:** Dynamic content updates that don't follow expected patterns",
